<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Pajang Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1568 CE – 1586 CE (approx.) · capital Pajang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A short-lived inland Javanese sultanate under Hadiwijaya that moved power from the coast to the interior between Demak and Mataram.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadiwijaya">Hadiwijaya</a>, also known as Jaka Tingkir, was a son-in-law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trenggana">Sultan Trenggana of Demak</a> and ruler of Pajang, near modern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/surakarta-sunanate">Surakarta</a>. In the succession war after Trenggana's death he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arya-penangsang">Arya Penangsang</a> with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ki-ageng-pemanahan">Ki Ageng Pemanahan</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panembahan-senapati">Sutawijaya</a>, and took the title sultan, the register dating his rule from 1568.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pajang moved the centre of Javanese power from the north coast to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> lands of the interior. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadiwijaya">Hadiwijaya</a> received the homage of the East Javanese lords at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/giri-kedaton">Giri Kedaton</a> and rewarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ki-ageng-pemanahan">Ki Ageng Pemanahan</a> with the land of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panembahan-senapati">Sutawijaya</a> grew powerful at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram</a> and defied Pajang. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadiwijaya">Hadiwijaya</a> died in 1582, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arya-pangiri">Arya Pangiri</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/demak-sultanate">Demak</a> seized the throne, but in 1586 Sutawijaya, allied with Hadiwijaya's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangeran-benawa">Pangeran Benawa</a>, deposed him. Pajang became a dependency of the Mataram Sultanate.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/pajang-sultanate.jpg" alt="Pajang Sultanate" width="320" height="167"><figcaption>Image: Fazoffic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Sultanate_of_Pajang_under_Joko_Tingkir.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1568 CE – 1586 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pajang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hadiwijaya, Arya Pangiri, Pangeran Benawa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Javanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Pajang, Pajang</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1577&amp;focus=pajang-sultanate" aria-label="Pajang Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7745 2461 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Pajang Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8065 2717L8063 2691M8065 2717L8052 2725"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8063 2691h0M8052 2725h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8065 2717h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8065 2717h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1577&amp;focus=pajang-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
